# Banking and the Reserve Bank of India

> The RBI steers the monetary system; commercial banks mobilise
> savings and multiply credit — together they form the economy's
> financial backbone.

[definition]
A **central bank** is the apex institution that issues currency,
banks for the government and the banking system, and controls money
and credit. India's central bank is the **Reserve Bank of India**,
established in **1935** under the **RBI Act, 1934**, and
**nationalised in 1949**.
[/definition]

## 1. Functions of the RBI

| Function | What it means |
| --- | --- |
| Issue of currency | Sole issuer of notes (₹2 and above); coins and ₹1 notes come from the Government |
| Banker to the government | Keeps accounts, manages public debt, arranges loans |
| Bankers' bank | Holds bank deposits, settles inter-bank claims, supervises banks |
| Lender of last resort | Banks in distress borrow from the RBI against collateral |
| Custodian of foreign exchange | Manages reserves and defends the rupee |
| Monetary authority | Controls money supply and credit (repo, CRR, and other tools) |
| Credit regulator | Sets priorities, margins, and lending norms |

## 2. Commercial Banks: Primary Functions

**Accepting deposits:**

- **Demand deposits** — current and savings accounts; withdrawable
  anytime, chequable.
- **Time deposits** — fixed and recurring deposits; locked for a
  term, earning higher interest.

**Advancing loans:** cash credit and overdrafts, demand and term
loans, discounting bills of exchange, and investment in government
securities.

**Secondary functions** include agency services (collecting cheques,
paying bills, buying and selling securities, fund transfers like
NEFT/UPI rails) and general utility services (lockers, letters of
credit, foreign exchange dealing).

## 3. Credit Creation and the Money Multiplier

Banks keep only a fraction of deposits as reserve and lend the rest;
loans return as new deposits, again partially lent — each round
shrinks by the **Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR)**, so the system multiplies
the original deposit:

@math
Credit\ Multiplier = \frac{1}{CRR},\qquad Total\ credit = Initial\ deposit \times \frac{1}{CRR}
@/math

[example title="1000 becomes 5000"]
With CRR 20%, a ₹1,000 deposit supports total deposits of
1,000 × (1/0.20) = **₹5,000** — the first ₹1,000 is real cash
(the monetary base); the other ₹4,000 is bank-created credit money.
A higher CRR shrinks the multiplier; lowering it expands credit.
[/example]

## 4. Nationalisation of Banks

- **1969** — 14 major private banks nationalised by bank size
  (deposits above ₹50 crore), taking banking to rural India.
- **1980** — 6 more banks nationalised, raising the public sector
  share of deposits above 90%.

Aim: social control over credit — priority-sector lending to
agriculture and small industry instead of concentration in urban,
large-business hands. The State Bank of India was earlier made a
public bank under the SBI Act, 1955.

## 5. The Indian Banking Structure

@diagram tree id="banking-structure" layout="tree" aria="Structure of Indian banking system"
  "Indian Banking" -> "RBI (apex regulator)"
  "Indian Banking" -> "Commercial Banks"
  "Indian Banking" -> "Cooperative Banks"
  "Commercial Banks" -> "Public Sector Banks"
  "Commercial Banks" -> "Private Sector Banks"
  "Commercial Banks" -> "Foreign Banks"
  "Commercial Banks" -> "Regional Rural Banks"
@/diagram

## 6. New-Age Banks

- **Payment banks** (recommended by the Nachiket Mor Committee,
  licensed from 2015): accept small deposits (then capped at
  ₹1 lakh per customer; limit later raised), offer remittances and
  payments — but **cannot lend** or issue credit cards. Examples:
  Airtel Payments Bank, Paytm Payments Bank (until 2024),
  India Post Payments Bank.
- **Small finance banks** (from 2015–16): full-service banks for
  financial inclusion — small loans, micro-credit, savings — with
  mandated priority-sector focus. Examples: AU Small Finance Bank,
  Ujjivan, Equitas.

[info title="Why payment banks cannot lend"]
Deposits are invested only in safe government securities up to a
prescribed limit, keeping payment-system money insulated from credit
risk — the same logic that keeps wallets out of the lending
business.
[/info]

## 7. Quick Revision Table

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| RBI established | 1935 (RBI Act 1934), nationalised 1949 |
| Lender of last resort | The RBI, to banks in distress |
| Money multiplier | 1/CRR; ₹1,000 at 20% CRR → ₹5,000 |
| Bank nationalisation | 1969 (14 banks); 1980 (6 banks) |
| Payment banks | Small deposits, remittances — no lending |
| Small finance banks | Inclusion-focused full-service banks |
| Demand vs time deposit | Withdrawable on demand vs locked for a term |

[summary]
The RBI governs currency, credit, and the payments architecture;
commercial banks turn deposits into loans and multiply money by
1/CRR. Nationalisation (1969, 1980) socialised credit, and payment
and small finance banks now extend the same architecture to the last
mile.
[/summary]
